  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CBHR2: Community Based Health and Resilience (focus on mother and child care)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">To improve sustainable maternal and child health and wellbeing, leading to the empowerment of women</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">a)	Improving women&#x2019;s health by buying into the overall national strategic approach to RMNCH by adopting and implementing the Safe Motherhood Action Groups (SMAGs) and Maternal Death Review (MDR) strategies within implementation area. Focus will be placed on the improvement of the health of pregnant and lactating women, as well as young children (first 1000 critical days), through community awareness and education that promote the utilization of the available Reproductive Maternal Neonatal and Child Health facilities and services, such as Child spacing (FP), adolescent reproductive health, ANC, delivery facilities, PNC, new-born care facilities and services and behaviour change strategies. The volunteers will be trained in CBHFA which is an approach to Health promotion activities to support communities in addressing the health challenges, the learning by doing approach. Project staff with District Health staff will look into the idea of supporting the communities with timely  implementation. The project will also finish the construction of one Mothers shelter and construct one new Mothers shelter.
b)	Related to the WASH component, the focus will be increasing access to safe drinking water through drilling or rehabilitating boreholes, improved sanitation through  promotion of construction of household latrines.. The project will endeavour to establish/reactivate water management committees before drilling/rehabilitation then train the committee, care takers, and pump menders, provision of maintenance kits and work in collaboration with the District council.. The project will train volunteers on Community Led Total Sanitation(CLTS) leading to construction of HHs latrines and certification of Open Defaecation Free(ODF) of the target communities. Volunteers will be trained in construction of model latrines that will be replicated in households across project implementation area and beyond.
c)	Empowering women by improving women&#x2019;s social and economic status, which will lead to improved economic status for easy access to health services.  . The power relations that impede women&apos;s attainment of healthy and fulfilling lives operate at many levels of society - from the most personal to the highly public. The project will focus on establishing group and individual IGAs through a micro-financing approach that targets pregnant and lactating mothers. This will be done through the involvement of the community in developing business plans for viable IGAs directly target the beneficiaries. Individuals and groups will be trained on business and financial management. Micro financing and revolving fund system will be established, and beneficiary groups will be aligned to MCDSS who will help with implementation and monitoring</narrative>
  </description>
